design §3 cross-host limit; fable J4 review F5; owner ruling 2026-10-25 \\\"document it\\\" (option a of the three-way: record, best-effort refusal, or common-lock design) | jolt lane (J4b) | documented limitation — the ledger row is the fix. A future common-lock design (option c) would supersede; the best-effort Node-claim refusal (option b) remains available as an enhancement if the accident ever occurs in practice. J4b's acceptance must not claim universal cross-runtime exclusion | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:536:| yang.php: a by-reference variadic parameter (`function f(&...$xs)`) is refused at lowering as `:variadic-reference` (C24, `G-variadic-by-ref`); by-value variadics, named extras and spread are supported | P3 plan section 5; Stage 0 gate (collab/1793020431000) D1; `e2e-test/refusals-are-named-test` | yang.php | P9 | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:537:| yang.php: a spread with string keys (`f(...['a' => 1])`) is refused at run time with `Error \\\"spread with string keys is not supported yet (yang.php known gap G-spread-string-keys)\\\"` (C25); PHP binds the keys as named arguments, so row 65 is not claimed at program level; fixture `:call-spread-string-key-gap` (oracle-only, pending the next shiva pass) | Stage 0 gate D2; `e2e-test/gate-fix-programs-test` | yang.php | P4 (row 65 lands with `pcall-arg-named`) | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:538:| yang.php: by-reference `foreach` keeps an ordinal and re-reads the location each iteration (C26, `G-foreach-iterator-registry`); the same-table clause of rows 25 and 26 is implemented, the copy-hit and unrelated clauses of row 25, the rebuild clause of row 26 and rows 54, 60 and 61 are not (`sort($a)` or `$a = [..]` inside the loop differ from PHP); `fe-next` is a linear scan per iteration; fixtures `:foreach-ref-sort-inside-loop` and `:foreach-ref-array-reassigned-in-loop` (oracle-only, pending) | Stage 0 gate D3; design 17.2 C26 | yang.php | P3-c (iterator registry, the P3 plan's S7.2 and S7.3), before P4-b lands row 29 | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:539:| yang.php: the vendored `PhpParser.g4` reads `fn($x)` as a call to a function named `fn` and `[fn($x) => ...]` as a key/value pair, and has no `foreach (... as $k => [$x, $y])`; the held fixtures `:call-dynamic-dim` and `:foreach-key-and-destructure` keep their recorded oracle values and flip on with a grammar patch (C27, `G-grammar-fn-item`) | Stage 0 gate D4; vendored grammar byte-identical to upstream `85e2dd17` | yang.php | a grammar-patch slice through the manifest `:patches` mechanism, regenerated on every target, before or inside P4 S0 | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:540:| yang.php: member calls go through one `php/method-call` seam (`getMessage`, `getCode`, `getPrevious` on Throwable; `bindTo`, `__invoke` on Closure); `bindTo` shares statics with the source closure (PHP copies them); `new` and classes are refused; the call protocol validates a named argument's name at entry, so in `f(1, z: 2, w: side())` `side()` runs where PHP stops at `z` (C28, `G-method-seam`); fixture `:call-unknown-named-later-arg-runs` (oracle-only, pending) | Stage 0 gate D5 and section 3; `e2e-test/gate-fix-programs-test` | yang.php | P4 S2 (class model) and P4 S4 (`pcall-begin/arg/enter` with `new`) | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:541:| yang.php: `var_dump` of a self-referencing array prints `*RECURSION*` at the re-entry (a visited path of instance-array cell ids) and `==`, `===` on cyclic arrays raise `Error \\\"Nesting level too deep - recursive dependency?\\\"` after a depth of 100 (C29, `G-recursion-guard`); the exact PHP texts are recorded by the fixtures `:ref-cycle-var-dump` and `:ref-cycle-compare` (pending the next shiva pass); `print_r`, `json_encode`, `var_export`, `serialize` are unsupported builtins | Stage 0 gate D7, adjudication X2; `prelude-test/gate-cycle-guard-test` | yang.php | the P4-seam oracle run pins the texts | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:542:| jolt: an invalid-UTF-8 key file fails closed