) instead of before it (`cDE`), because cleanup surrounds only the body and not parameter initialization | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | P4 S6 (cover parameter initialization in the unwinding cleanup so every acquired parameter is released on failure) | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:571:| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaks an earlier parameter's acquisition: with `function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`, `catch (TypeError $e)`, then `$e = null; unset($a); echo 'E';`, PHP prints `cDE` (the freed trace releases its copy, `unset` drops the last holder) and yang.php prints `cED` (the acquisition of `$a` made before `$b` failed is never released, because cleanup surrounds only the body, not parameter initialization). Without `$e = null` both print `cED`, PHP because the trace holds the argument (corner C30), yang.php because of this leak | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5, restated by the 17.3 ruling of 2026-10-28; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | P4 S6 (cover parameter initialization in the unwinding cleanup so every acquired parameter is released on failure); fixture `:decl-byref-later-rejection-releases-earlier-param` (`#{:p4-s6}`) | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:573:| yang.php: compile-time default validation was incomplete (a float default for an `int` property or parameter, arrays and signed literals passed lowering, and `:unknown` escaped whenever a class, object, callable or iterable member existed); closed in P4 S6: `init-kind` classifies int, float, string, bool, null, array and signed numeric defaults from the syntax tree, `kind-fits?` holds them to the declared type (an int widens to float; callable members accept strings and arrays), and only a default that names a constant is checked when the object or parameter is installed | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` default checks | yang.php | closed in P4 S6; `compile-errors-are-named-test` entries and fixture `:decl-signed-literal-defaults-accepted` | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:574:| yang.php: a later rejected by-reference argument leaked an earlier parameter's acquisition (`function f(D &$a, int &$b)`, `$a = new D`, `$b = 'bad'`: PHP prints `cDE` once the trace is freed, yang.php printed `cED`); closed in P4 S6: declared parameter types are checked in the body's prologue (`php/check-param-cell!`), inside the cleanup that releases every parameter cell, and a function with a typed parameter always takes the unwinding cleanup path. Without freeing the exception both print `cED` (corner C30) | P4 S5 independent review (codex gpt-6.1-sol round 2, P2) and Architect sign-off condition 5, restated by the 17.3 ruling of 2026-10-28; `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` parameter initialization | yang.php | closed in P4 S6; fixture `:decl-byref-later-rejection-releases-earlier-param` (enabled) | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:577:| yang.php: destructuring by reference into a property (`[&$o->p] = $a`, row 47) is not lowered: the destructuring pattern accepts variable and element targets only; fixture `:obj-row47-destructure-by-ref-into-typed-property` is authored and gated by `:p4-row47` | checkpoint B audit (fixture package); `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc` `pattern-of` | yang.php | P4 S7 or a destructuring slice before checkpoint C (the gate lifts when the pattern accepts property targets) | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:578:| yang.php: `exit`/`die` is refused (`:constant`), so row 51 (`exit` inside a destructor proceeds to shutdown, corner C9) cannot run; fixture `:obj-row51-exit-in-destructor` is authored and gated by `:p4-exit` | checkpoint B audit (fixture package); design row 51 | yang.php | P4 S7 (the exit escape and the pending-exception destruct halt) | P2 |\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs_p5.cljc:2:  \\\"P5 request-context fixtures. Expectations are hand-derived from PHP 8.3;\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs_p5.cljc:25:  (merge {:id id, :group :request-context, :source source, :out out,\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/request_context_test.clj:1:(ns yang.php.antlr.request-context-test\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/request_context_test.clj:38:(deftest request-context-fixtures-test\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/prelude_test.cljc:82:;; The array value (P2 step 1): direct prelude tests, on every host\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs.cljc:2:  \\\"PHP fixtures for slices P1 and P2: source, expected output, the oracle\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs.cljc:5:   function, static-variable, warning and `@` groups and the P2 array groups\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs.cljc:857:   `[0]` columns are P2's and the object columns P4's.\\\"\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:3:Status: Designed 2026-10-17. Slices P1 and P2 (section 15) are implemented on\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:4:the `yang.php.antlr` route (P2: arrays, key coercion, `foreach` by value, the\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:137:#### 3.2.1 Reads, `isset`, `empty`, `unset` and builtins (P2)\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2022:| P2    | arrays: the value, key coercion, | the key-coercion fixture table;   |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2216:| C6 internal         | Engine: `sort`, `array_push`,       | restricted; P2   |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2287:| C15 iterator        | Engine: the exact outcome of        | restricted; P2   |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2294:|                     | in P2 or stay listed here           |                  |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2541:|                             | criteria 1 to 5 are direct fixtures (P1, P2    |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2562:  dependencies), five P2s (among them PHP semantics against the 8.3 oracle,\\ndocs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md:620:| An answer that failed the outcome-map convention reached the classifier and woke with `:status nil` | An answer that fails `stream/validate-outcome`'s shape validation — a non-map, a missing or unqualified `:dao.stream/outcome`, a declared outcome missing its required keys — wakes as `:dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer`: terminal, out of `:waiting`, no `:advance` | \\\"An answer the library cannot interpret is terminal data, never an exception and never a wait\\\"; a `:status nil` wake is unnameable by any host branch. A well-formed outcome outside the declared set stays terminal under its own keyword, as before (W1 review, P2) |\\ndocs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md:621:| A poll-time throw — a `:stream` resolved from a stale store record that fails its protocol, a defective transport — escaped `check` as a host dispatch error and voided the sweep | The per-entry poll is wrapped in a catch-all and the throw folds into `:dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer`; the entry leaves `:waiting`, because an entry whose handle throws cannot be trusted to poll again | The engine let the throw escape; a library serving several consumers returns data instead, and re-parking such an entry would spin the sweep into the same throw every round (W1 review, P2) |\\ndocs/design/dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md:622:| A resolver throw escaped the engine's sweep and voided it | Unchanged, now stated as the contract on resolvers: resolvers must not throw, and a throw voids the sweep — `check` does not return, and re-threading the previous waitset re-polls every entry, re-appending writers that already appended (`check` docstring) | Faithful to the engine. Catching around host resolver composition would hide host defects inside a diagnostic the library cannot ground; the no-dropped-state law is documented as the resolvers' obligation instead (W1 review, P2) |\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:246:  Outcome: no P0-P2; verdict \\\"ready to commit\\\". One P3 applied in the same\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:449:  the conve